Investigation of an intermittent failure in tests/fm-home-summary-refresh.test.sh
("a dead publication lock wedged publication", the "publication remains
single-flight across watcher restart" case) observed while running this suite
concurrently with 7 other heavy test suites.

Timeline:
- Target commit, run concurrently with 7 other suites: FAILED twice
  ("not ok - a dead publication lock wedged publication").
- Base commit (252cad7...), run alone: PASSED.
- Target commit, re-run alone (no concurrent load): PASSED twice (once via
  `bash -x` trace, once via bin/fm-test-run.sh).
- Base and target, re-run SIMULTANEOUSLY under matched synthetic CPU load
  (6 busy-loop processes pinned at ~72% CPU each, to reproduce the same
  contention as the original 8-suite concurrent run): BOTH FAILED identically
  with the same "not ok - a dead publication lock wedged publication" message.
  Debug output showed the underlying lock directory left in an inconsistent/
  orphaned state (an owner directory present, primary lock symlink missing or
  a stray owner directory) on BOTH commits - i.e. a pre-existing race in the
  lock acquire/reclaim directory dance under heavy CPU starvation, present
  before this change and not introduced by it.

Conclusion: this is flaky, load-sensitive test/infrastructure behavior that
predates this branch, not a regression introduced by the lock-identity change.
It reproduces identically on the base commit under matched load and passes
reliably on both commits when the machine is not under heavy contention.
Reported as an informational (no-op) finding, not a blocker for this change.
